    "md5": "6ee81780b6ab95415721901b24ec1f95",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1555,
    "chunk_text": "Third, cleansing the enforcement chain. Disanayake\u2019s tough rhetoric must be matched by real institutional reform \u2014 independent audits of police and customs, body-worn cameras, rotation of personnel in high-risk posts, and protection for whistle-blowers.\n\nFifth, maritime and regional cooperation. Sri Lanka\u2019s location on Indian Ocean trafficking routes makes it a relay hub. Sustained coordination with India, Pakistan, and Gulf states on maritime surveillance and precursor-chemical control is vital.\n\nSixth, avoiding politicisation. Naming the political-criminal nexus is bold, but enforcing it even-handedly will test the administration\u2019s credibility. Selective targeting or perceived vendettas would quickly erode public trust.\n\nSeventh, sustaining momentum. Anti-drug drives often fade once the media spotlight moves on. Institutionalisation \u2014 not campaign-style enthusiasm \u2014 must ensure that the mission outlives political cycles.\n\nTo know whether the \u201cwar on drugs\u201d has turned into a long-term strategy, Sri Lanka will need transparent benchmarks:\n\nAt least a 50 percent increase in convictions of top-tier traffickers within two years.\n\nAnnual public reporting on assets seized and proceeds forfeited under the Prevention of Money Laundering Act.\n\nExpansion of voluntary treatment capacity by at least 30% within 18 months.\n\nReduction in youth-initiation rates in Western and Southern Provinces, measured through NDDCB data.\n\nZero tolerance for internal corruption\u2014with mandatory dismissal and prosecution of implicated officers."
