    "chunk_text": "\u201cFor the first time, Sri Lanka is building a single, technologically empowered platform to detect, analyse and respond to environmental crimes in real time. This is not merely enforcement, it is a national shield for our forests, wildlife and biodiversity.\u201d\n\nBy centralising information and maintaining continuous surveillance, the operations unit aims to detect threats as they emerge\u00a0 and even before they occur. This includes quicker mobilisation during wildfires, illegal land clearing and timber smuggling.\n\nDr. Kariyawasam said that the heightened monitoring will act as a strong deterrent, reducing the space available for offenders to operate.\n\nThe launch aligns with the government\u2019s ongoing environmental policy reforms, which include reviewing outdated laws, aligning national regulations with international commitments, inviting public participation, and drafting new legislation where required.\n\nA committee of experts is already overseeing these reforms under the Environment Ministry\u2019s supervision. The \u201cWana Surakuma\u201d unit is expected to serve as the operational backbone of the wider policy transformation.\n\nOfficials emphasise that the establishment of this integrated unit reflects a long-term national commitment to safeguarding Sri Lanka\u2019s natural wealth.\n\n\u201cIf we are to secure a sustainable future for our children, we must act decisively today. \u2018Wana Surakuma\u2019 marks a turning point,\u00a0 where technology, expertise and public cooperation come together to defend our environment.\u201d\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s Northern Gateway: Economic promise and geopolitical power in the Indian Ocean\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
