    "chunk_size_bytes": 1833,
    "chunk_text": "The CEA notes that the move is already creating behavioural shifts among consumers. \u201cWe are seeing more people bringing their own reusable bags. That\u2019s exactly what this policy was designed to achieve,\u201d Rajapaksha said.\n\nRajapaksha explained that while recycling is indeed a vital part of waste management, it\u2019s not always practical for LDPE bags. \u201cThese thin plastics are hard to collect, clean, and recycle economically,\u201d he said. \u201cBecause the cost of producing recycled pellets is almost the same as making new plastic, there\u2019s very little commercial incentive. So, expecting to recycle all 20 million bags used daily in Sri Lanka is simply unrealistic.\u201d\n\nThe CEA Director General warned that so-called \u201cbiodegradable\u201d plastics often mislead the public. \u201cMost of these contain additives like calcium carbonate that break the bag into microplastics, not into harmless organic matter,\u201d he explained. \u201cThat makes the problem worse \u2014 not better.\u201d\n\nTruly eco-friendly bio-based plastics do exist, but their production cost is much higher. \u201cNo business will give away a Rs. 10\u201315 bag for free,\u201d Rajapaksha noted.\n\nDoes charging money mean the bag quality will improve or deteriorate?\n\n\u201cThe price is not about the bag quality \u2014 it\u2019s about discouraging unnecessary use,\u201d Rajapaksha said. \u201cWhen you pay for something, even a small amount, you think twice before taking it.\u201d\n\nThe first two days of implementation have proven this behavioural shift: consumers are bringing reusable bags, and daily plastic waste has already dropped.\n\n\u201cPrimary packaging \u2014 the first layer that holds the product \u2014 is mandatory. But the second bag given at the cashier is not,\u201d he explained. \u201cThat\u2019s why the rule specifically targets these \u2018secondary\u2019 or \u2018thin\u2019 bags, which are optional.\u201d"
