    "chunk_text": "\u201cPrimary packaging \u2014 the first layer that holds the product \u2014 is mandatory. But the second bag given at the cashier is not,\u201d he explained. \u201cThat\u2019s why the rule specifically targets these \u2018secondary\u2019 or \u2018thin\u2019 bags, which are optional.\u201d\n\nThe CEA maintains that this measure is about changing public habits rather than generating revenue. \u201cWhen people stop expecting free bags and start carrying their own, that\u2019s when real progress happens,\u201d Rajapaksha emphasised.\n\nSocial media, too, has played a surprising role. \u201cWe\u2019re glad to see so many citizens expressing support for this move. Constructive dialogue and shared responsibility can turn this small step into a national transformation,\u201d he said.\n\nAs Sri Lanka continues to battle the mounting plastic waste crisis \u2014 with over 20 million \u201cSili Sili\u201d bags used daily before the ban \u2014 the decision to charge for shopping bags marks a turning point. Whether it leads to lasting behavioural change will depend on sustained enforcement, business cooperation, and the public\u2019s willingness to adapt.\n\n\u201cIf we all take this seriously, the benefits will go far beyond cleaner streets,\u201d Rajapaksha concluded. \u201cIt\u2019s about reshaping how we live with the environment \u2014 one small bag at a time.\u201d\n\nNew national operations unit to shield Sri Lanka\u2019s forests to be unveiled today\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
