    "md5": "e31ea8564d7d505cdb3043a7bd473e1e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "He noted that partnerships like Bosch\u2019s demonstrated how international investment could accelerate national development by transferring knowledge, building ecosystems, and inspiring new generations of innovators. The Minister called for continued focus on research, development, and local innovation to ensure Sri Lanka evolved from a consumer to a creator of technology.\n\nNeumann expressed confidence that Bosch would continue to play a central role in driving innovation in Sri Lanka and enhance livelihoods of those it impacts.\n\nIn 2024, Bosch reported sales of 90.3 billion euros and employs around 417,900 employees, operating across four key sectors: mobility, industrial technology, consumer goods, and energy and building technology. The company\u2019s strategy focuses on using technology to help shape universal trends such as automation, electrification, digitalisation, connectivity, and an orientation to sustainability. In this context, its broad diversification across regions and industries continues to strengthen its innovative capacity and long-term resilience.\n\nDescribing Sri Lanka\u2019s market as a key market in Bosch\u2019s regional growth story, Bosch Group India and South Asia President and Bosch Ltd., Managing Director Guruprasad Mudlapur said that the company aimed to \u201cmake things better and bigger\u201d for the country\u2019s industrial sector through sustained investment and collaboration.\n\nHe explained that while global markets remained uncertain, with weak growth in Europe and shifting trade conditions, emerging economies like Sri Lanka offered real potential. \u201cWe are very seriously working towards aligning our growth and vision with the Sri Lankan Government\u2019s vision of ensuring sustainable growth, technology, digitisation and innovation. We believe a lot of what we do gels well with the Government\u2019s vision of how the industry should perform in Sri Lanka.\u201d"
